# MeetingMinutes configuration. Copy to .env (scripts/install.sh does this for you).
# Everything is optional; the defaults run the site on 127.0.0.1:8787.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- web server
# Bind address and port. For a public IP either:
#   - serve HTTPS directly: HOST=0.0.0.0, PORT=8443 and the TLS_* files below, or
#   - keep 127.0.0.1 and put Caddy/nginx in front (see README).
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=8787
# Built-in HTTPS (no reverse proxy). scripts/make-cert.sh <public-ip> creates these.
# TLS_CERT_FILE=data/tls/server.crt
# TLS_KEY_FILE=data/tls/server.key
# With built-in HTTPS, local GPU workers use this loopback-only HTTP port.
# INTERNAL_PORT=8788
# On the standard port (PORT=443, PUBLIC_URL without a port), the earlier port
# can send people over, keeping links working; workers enrolled with the old
# address keep working too. Plain http:// can be sent over as well. Ports below
# 1024 need: net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start (see README), or root.
# REDIRECT_PORTS=8443
# HTTP_REDIRECT_PORT=80
# The URL people use to reach the site. Enables Secure cookies + HSTS when https,
# and is used for invite links.
PUBLIC_URL=
# Host names the site answers to. Requests for any other host, such as the bare
# IP address, get "421 Misdirected Request" (programs on this machine may still
# use localhost). Default: the host name of PUBLIC_URL; empty with no
# PUBLIC_URL accepts any host. List several separated by commas.
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=minutes.example.com
# How people reach the administrator when they have no account or forgot their
# password; shown on the sign-in page. An email address becomes a mailto link.
# e.g. ADMIN_CONTACT=it-help@example.com   or   ADMIN_CONTACT=Slack #it-help
ADMIN_CONTACT=
# Export summaries straight into Google Docs: the client ID of a Google OAuth
# client of type "Web application", with PUBLIC_URL as an authorised JavaScript
# origin and the Google Drive API enabled (see README, "Exporting to Google
# Docs"). No client secret is needed. Empty: summaries are copied with their
# formatting, to paste into a new Google Doc.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# Set to true when behind a reverse proxy so client IPs (rate limiting) come from X-Forwarded-For.
TRUST_PROXY=false
# Where the SQLite database and media files live.
DATA_DIR=data
SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30
# Largest file accepted (4096 = 4 GB). Files are sent in chunks, so this is not
# limited by memory.
MAX_UPLOAD_MB=4096
# Upload chunk size. Behind a proxy, keep it below the proxy's body limit (Cloudflare: 100 MB).
UPLOAD_CHUNK_MB=32
# Longest recording a worker decodes, in hours. A small file that expands into
# more audio than this (a crafted or corrupt file) is rejected instead of
# exhausting the worker's memory.
MAX_AUDIO_HOURS=8

# Keep only compact audio: some days after a meeting is transcribed, a worker
# replaces its original with mono Opus at MEDIA_COMPACT_KBPS (32 kbit/s is about
# 14 MB per hour) and drops any video. Cannot be undone. Defaults: Admin ->
# System -> Keeping recordings overrides the first two.
MEDIA_COMPACT=false
MEDIA_COMPACT_AFTER_DAYS=7
MEDIA_COMPACT_KBPS=32
# A worker that should not do this work (another one does): MM_MEDIA_JOBS=0
# Workers on other machines download a small audio-only copy (mono Opus,
# 48 kbit/s), which this server makes with the worker's ffmpeg. Another ffmpeg:
# FFMPEG_BIN=/usr/bin/ffmpeg
# Speaker recognition by voice (see README, "Recognising speakers by voice").
# Cosine similarity of voice fingerprints, from -1 to 1: at or above MATCH a
# speaker is named after a known voice; from SUGGEST up to MATCH the name is
# only suggested. Raise them if people are confused with one another; lower
# MATCH if the same person is often only suggested.
VOICE_MATCH_THRESHOLD=0.6
VOICE_SUGGEST_THRESHOLD=0.45
# Seconds of speech a speaker needs before their voice is remembered or compared.
VOICE_MIN_SPEECH_SEC=5

# Shared secret between the backend and GPU workers. Generated on first start
# into data/worker.token when left empty.
WORKER_TOKEN=

# ----------------------------------------------------------- summary model
# Who writes summaries: local (a model on your own GPU, or any model server you
# name below) or api (a hosted model: Anthropic, OpenAI, OpenRouter or Google
# Gemini). These are defaults: administrators can change all of them in
# Admin → Summary model, and what they save there wins until they reset it.
SUMMARY_PROVIDER=local
# With SUMMARY_PROVIDER=api: the provider (anthropic, openai, openrouter, gemini)
# and the model; empty uses the provider's suggested model (claude-haiku-5-5 for
# Anthropic). Transcripts are then sent to that provider.
SUMMARY_API_PROVIDER=anthropic
SUMMARY_API_MODEL=
# The provider's API key. Only the one you use is needed; it stays on this server
# (never stored in the database or shown in the browser).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=
# Context to use when the provider does not report the model's (Anthropic does);
# longer transcripts are read in parts. And how many summaries run at once.
SUMMARY_API_CONTEXT_TOKENS=128000
SUMMARY_API_CONCURRENCY=2
# The local model server: Ollama (scripts/llm.sh serve), or an OpenAI-compatible
# one (vLLM, llama.cpp, LM Studio, SGLang) with LLM_API=openai.
LLM_API=ollama
# Ollama listens on this machine only (scripts/llm.sh refuses any other address):
# it has no authentication, and only the web server talks to it.
LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434
# Qwen3.8 27B, 3-bit (Q3_K_M, about 13 GB). Download: scripts/llm.sh pull
LLM_MODEL=batiai/qwen3.8-27b:q3
# Prompt + answer, in tokens. Longer transcripts are read in parts, never cut off.
LLM_CONTEXT_TOKENS=65536
# Let the model think before writing (slower; sometimes better on hard meetings).
LLM_THINK=false
# Seconds the model stays in GPU memory after a summary (0 = free it for transcription at once).
LLM_KEEP_ALIVE_SEC=0
# Longest a summary may take, and how many run at once.
LLM_TIMEOUT_SEC=3600
LLM_CONCURRENCY=1
# GPUs Ollama may use (nvidia-smi numbers, e.g. 1). Empty = all.
LLM_GPUS=
# Where scripts/llm.sh keeps Ollama (a few GB) and its models (about 13 GB for
# the default). Move them off a home directory with a quota, e.g.
#   OLLAMA_PREFIX=~/tmp/ollama
#   OLLAMA_MODELS=~/tmp/.ollama/models
# Empty: ~/.local/ollama and ~/.ollama/models.
OLLAMA_PREFIX=
OLLAMA_MODELS=
# Bearer token, only for an OpenAI-compatible server started with an API key.
LLM_API_KEY=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- worker
# Hugging Face token (read access) for pyannote speaker diarization. Accept the
# conditions of pyannote/speaker-diarization-community-1 with the same account.
HF_TOKEN=
ASR_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen3-ASR-1.7B
# transformers (default) or vllm (faster, needs `scripts/install.sh --vllm`).
ASR_BACKEND=transformers
ASR_BATCH_SIZE=16
DIARIZATION_MODEL=pyannote/speaker-diarization-community-1
MAX_SEGMENT_SEC=30
# Models are loaded onto the GPU only while there is work, then the GPU process
# exits and all VRAM is released. Seconds to wait for another job before exiting:
MODEL_KEEPALIVE_SEC=0
# GPUs to run workers on (comma separated). Empty = every GPU nvidia-smi reports.
WORKER_GPUS=
# CPU priority of the worker (scripts/worker.sh runs it under `nice`): 10 by
# default, so the web server on the same machine stays responsive while a
# recording is decoded or its speakers are separated on the CPU. 19 is the
# lowest priority; 0 runs the worker without nice.
# WORKER_NICE=10
# Free GPU memory a session needs: both models (MIN_FREE_VRAM_GB), or only
# speaker diarization when transcription goes through an API (MIN_FREE_VRAM_API_GB).
# MIN_FREE_VRAM_GB=8
# MIN_FREE_VRAM_API_GB=3
# Backend URL as seen from the worker. Default: http://127.0.0.1:PORT, or
# http://127.0.0.1:INTERNAL_PORT when TLS_CERT_FILE is set.
# MM_SERVER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787

# ------------------------------------------------------- transcription API
# Where speech is recognised: local (Qwen3-ASR on the worker's GPU) or api (a
# hosted model; the worker still separates speakers and sends each speech
# segment, at most MAX_SEGMENT_SEC long). Defaults: administrators can change
# them in Admin -> Transcription, and what they save there wins until reset.
ASR_ENGINE=local
# openrouter (https://openrouter.ai/api/v1) or openai (any OpenAI-compatible
# /audio/transcriptions server: OpenAI, Groq, a vLLM serving Qwen3-ASR).
ASR_API_PROVIDER=openrouter
ASR_API_MODEL=qwen/qwen3-asr-1.7b
# OpenAI-compatible servers only, ending in /v1. Empty: https://api.openai.com/v1.
ASR_API_BASE_URL=
# Keys, read by the worker only (never by the web server or a browser).
# OpenRouter: OPENROUTER_API_KEY, else ASR_API_KEY. OpenAI-compatible: ASR_API_KEY, else OPENAI_API_KEY.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
ASR_API_KEY=
# Segments sent at the same time, and the longest one request may take.
ASR_API_CONCURRENCY=4
ASR_API_TIMEOUT_SEC=120

# ------------------------------------------------------- speaker separation
# Who separates speakers: local (pyannote on the worker's GPU, needs HF_TOKEN),
# cpu (sherpa-onnx on the worker's CPU: free, nothing uploaded, less accurate)
# or pyannoteai (hosted, paid; the whole recording is uploaded). A default:
# Admin -> Transcription overrides it. With cpu or pyannoteai and ASR_ENGINE=api,
# a worker needs no GPU, CUDA or torch (scripts/install.sh --no-gpu).
DIARIZATION_ENGINE=local
# CPU separation: threads (default: the number of cores, at most 8) and the
# clustering threshold for an automatic speaker count (larger: fewer speakers).
# DIARIZATION_CPU_THREADS=4
# DIARIZATION_CPU_THRESHOLD=0.5
# Read by workers only (dashboard.pyannote.ai).
PYANNOTEAI_API_KEY=
# Where workers keep downloaded models (CPU separation and voice fingerprints, ~45 MB). Default: DATA_DIR/models.
# MM_MODEL_DIR=
